 :question/is-example false :question/problem "Calculate the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{4}+{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator and canceling any common factors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{4}+{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}({x}^{2}+x+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{{x}^{2}}}{\\cancel{{x}^{2}}({x}^{2}+x+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+x+2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we set the denominator equal to zero:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+x+2=0\n$$\n\nThis equation does not have any real solutions because the discriminant $D$ is negative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & =1-4(1)(2) \\\\  & =-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has no vertical asymptotes." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-113545/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b0b57da-f9af-5fab-a337-51ee4cb28f9e"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-113545"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-39934/selection/f2d0f5c0341066fddb62cf1064d5cdf4169d5bb9201989c85ce2b2a732fb48b4" :answer/id #uuid "57262577-5ada-5fee-b3d6-4e7457b374df" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=-2,\\,x=2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-39934/selection/bdc927ca474c973def251df57d8c9c444ccb057002e142ea9e569787746fd7c9" :answer/id #uuid "714e9903-5139-5497-a5a2-e133e2c57e34" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$x=-7$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-39934/selection/e5d103e2c07ae759d9eddce65c552ccfa6a5a00acf0aea2f18942b9a819f2577" :answer/id #uuid "20bbc078-c8db-55ef-9abd-bb183c53b3a7" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The function has no vertical asymptotes" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-39934/selection/517b13fa7d7411493417798333c215e538be20515774e23158b4c71536604686" :answer/id #uuid "0dd8c48a-48dd-543e-a81f-91aa6a23409a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$x=0$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-39934/selection/1288db97e8c08fe1e59253bc39f36416fd21e4daf73730ee4bb5aebee1b12042" :answer/id #uuid "48e6d0f6-bb38-5af3-bc2e-8fd38f543d65" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$x=2$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-39934/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "1ff296ab-3b26-5c48-8f90-3081b1849439" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-39934/selection/e5d103e2c07ae759d9eddce65c552ccfa6a5a00acf0aea2f18942b9a819f2577" :answer-field/choices ["q-39934/selection/f2d0f5c0341066fddb62cf1064d5cdf4169d5bb9201989c85ce2b2a732fb48b4" "q-39934/selection/bdc927ca474c973def251df57d8c9c444ccb057002e142ea9e569787746fd7c9" "q-39934/selection/e5d103e2c07ae759d9eddce65c552ccfa6a5a00acf0aea2f18942b9a819f2577" "q-39934/selection/517b13fa7d7411493417798333c215e538be20515774e23158b4c71536604686" "q-39934/selection/1288db97e8c08fe1e59253bc39f36416fd21e4daf73730ee4bb5aebee1b12042"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-39934" :question/id #uuid "9d19bd23-7337-51bb-8b7d-7464ffe77faa" :question/math-academy-id "q-39934" :question/is-example false :question/problem "Determine the vertical asymptotes of the function $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}+3x}{{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}+8x}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator and canceling any common factors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{2}+3x}{{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}+8x} \\\\  & =\\frac{x(x+3)}{x({x}^{2}+2x+8)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{x}(x+3)}{\\cancel{x}({x}^{2}+2x+8)} \\\\  & =\\frac{x+3}{{x}^{2}+2x+8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we set the denominator equal to zero:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2x+8=0\n$$\n\nThis equation does not have any real solutions because the discriminant $D$ is negative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & ={2}^{2}-4(1)(8) \\\\  & =-28\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has no vertical asymptotes." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-39934/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b0b57da-f9af-5fab-a337-51ee4cb28f9e"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-39934"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]}]
